    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1743142289059261.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-03-08-2025-03-08-0100",
    "chunk_index": 100,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "b802735aacace0e20a019d73005e07fd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1382,
    "chunk_text": "In addition, if you take maintenance, it is adjudicated in the Magistrate\u2019s Court. But, if a Muslim woman wants to get maintenance, she first has to go to the Quazi Court, get an order and then get it enforced by the Magistrate\u2019s Court. So, I would suggest that you make provision for a direct mechanism, through which a Muslim woman also can go direct to the Magistrate\u2019s Court and seek maintenance.\n\nHon. Minister, with regard to the Judiciary, I have to bring up an issue, which does not come under your Ministerial Consultative Committee, but is a matter of national importance.\n\n1629 1630\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db8\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\n\nopportunity to provide a repudiation or an explanation as regards to the complaint. As you know, there are legitimate expectations with regard to everybody, but when it comes to judges, we do not have that. And also, when a seniormost judge is overlooked - they also have families - the society looks at them in a different way. So, there needs to be a collective process where a seniormost judge, who feels that his name has unjustly not been recommended, could place his grievance before the Chief Justice and the Constitutional Council and the merits and demerits of his grievance would be considered before depriving that seniormost judge the promotion. Sir, I wanted myself to be clear in this because this deals with the Judiciary."
  },
