    "chunk_size_bytes": 1565,
    "chunk_text": "opportunity to provide a repudiation or an explanation as regards to the complaint. As you know, there are legitimate expectations with regard to everybody, but when it comes to judges, we do not have that. And also, when a seniormost judge is overlooked - they also have families - the society looks at them in a different way. So, there needs to be a collective process where a seniormost judge, who feels that his name has unjustly not been recommended, could place his grievance before the Chief Justice and the Constitutional Council and the merits and demerits of his grievance would be considered before depriving that seniormost judge the promotion. Sir, I wanted myself to be clear in this because this deals with the Judiciary.\n\nAnother important matter, Hon. Minister, is the estate wages. Today, an estate worker gets a daily wage of Rs. 1,350. Socially and economically, the most deprived in Sri Lanka are the estate workers. I think at the Wages Board, the last regime tried to increase it to Rs. 2,000. Members of your party also were mebers of the Wages Board. I would urge you to speak to the Minister of Plantation and Community Infrastructure and the Minister of Labour and see that a wage of Rs. 2,000 is given to them because, always, you have estate owners milking profits and not giving the estate employee that Rs. 2,000 wage.\n\nThat is justly due. And, I believe that the estate women are the most affected. I started my politics from the Ceylon Workers\u2019 Congress. Therefore, the estate worker is always a subject which I would touch upon."
  },
